-- The ePrivacy Directive (Directive 2002/58/EC), Publications Office consolidated version of 19.12.2009.
-- NOUNS MODULE: the enumerations that more than one module uses. DECLARE and nothing else: no rules, no #EVAL.
-- Every other module imports this one. A fact the person can state (yes/no, a number, a date) is a plain input
-- of the rule that needs it and is not declared here.

-- The terms of the Directive that Article 2 itself defines, and the terms it uses but borrows.
-- (Art 2: ‘user’, ‘traffic data’, ‘location data’, ‘communication’, ‘consent’, ‘value added service’,
-- ‘electronic mail’ and ‘personal data breach’ are defined by the Directive; the others are used in the text
-- without a definition of their own, so they come from Directive 95/46/EC or Directive 2002/21/EC.)
DECLARE `Directive term` IS ONE OF
    `user`
    `traffic data`
    `location data`
    `communication`
    `consent`
    `value added service`
    `electronic mail`
    `personal data breach`
    `subscriber`
    `personal data`
    `data subject`
    `publicly available electronic communications service`
    `public communications network`
    `terminal equipment`
    `direct marketing`

-- The means of sending a marketing message, as Article 13(1) lists them, and every other means.
DECLARE `Marketing means` IS ONE OF
    `an automated calling and communication system without human intervention`
    `a facsimile machine`
    `an electronic mail message`
    `another means of communication`

-- Article 13(3): national legislation chooses between two options for the cases that are not in Article 13(1) or (2).
DECLARE `Article 13(3) national option` IS ONE OF
    `consent of the subscriber or user is required`
    `subscribers or users who do not wish to receive the communications are protected`
    `national legislation has not been stated`

-- Article 6(5): the activities for which traffic data may be handled; and every other activity.
DECLARE `Traffic data activity` IS ONE OF
    `billing`
    `traffic management`
    `customer enquiries`
    `fraud detection`
    `marketing electronic communications services`
    `providing a value added service`
    `another activity`

-- Article 15(1): the provisions whose rights and obligations a Member State may restrict; and every other provision.
DECLARE `Restrictable provision` IS ONE OF
    `Article 5`
    `Article 6`
    `Article 8(1)`
    `Article 8(2)`
    `Article 8(3)`
    `Article 8(4)`
    `Article 9`
    `another provision of this Directive`

-- Article 15(1): the purposes for which a restriction may be adopted; and every other purpose.
DECLARE `Restriction purpose` IS ONE OF
    `national security (State security)`
    `defence`
    `public security`
    `the prevention, investigation, detection and prosecution of criminal offences or of unauthorised use of the electronic communication system`
    `another purpose`

-- The parties and the acts of the regulative rules (PARTY ... MUST / SHANT ... WITHIN ... LEST). A regulative rule of this
-- encoding has the type DEONTIC `Party under the Directive` `Act under the Directive`. Where the text gives no deadline,
-- the rule has none; where it says "without undue delay" the deadline is a number the caller states.
DECLARE `Party under the Directive` IS ONE OF
    `the provider`
    `the sender`
    `the person intercepting`

DECLARE `Act under the Directive` IS ONE OF
    `inform the subscribers of the risk`
    `notify the authority of the personal data breach`
    `notify the subscriber or individual of the personal data breach`
    `erase the traffic data or make it anonymous`
    `send direct marketing electronic mail`
    `intercept the communication`
    `provide the authority with the information`
